I am going to take a guess in that you are also creating a new container each time to that scene. So you will need to loop through all containers as well (assuming you named them 'container'):
JavaScript:
const s = SceneManager._scene._spriteset.children;
for (let i = 0; i < s.length; i++) {
  if (s[i].name === 'container') {
    for (let j = 0; i < s.children.length; j++) {
      const c = s[i].children[j];
      if (c.name === 'house') c.clearRect(0, 0, 200, 200);
    }
  }
}
If you must clean the last one you put on there first, then you need to find the last child and destroy or clear it one by one since the loop will happen instantly:
JavaScript:
const s = SceneManager._scene._spriteset.children;
const child = s[s.length - 1].children;
child[child.length].destroy();
or
JavaScript:
const s = SceneManager._scene._spriteset.children;
const child = s[s.length - 1].children;
child[child.length - 1].bitmap.clearRect(0,0,200,200);
With this code, you need to do length - 2, and then length - 3...until you cycle through everything. Doing it with a loop will just do it all at once, even in reverse. It will be too quick for the player to see it happening in reverse (unless you set up some wait times inside an event, and that is a whole other conversation).
It becomes harder to deal with because you can't always keep track of what you added and you add more guesswork to your workload. Destroy() might be better if you know you aren't going to use that bitmap anymore.
To be able to draw on the same bitmap, as I stated earlier, you have to find that bitmap and then reference the context.
Let's say you use the script above which created a new container and a new bitmap with the house. You then cleared it (not destroy) and you want to draw something else on it - you need to find that specific container and bitmap before you can draw on it .
In this example, I know that the last child I added is the container and the last child I added to the container is the house so I just need to do .length - 1 for each of them:
JavaScript:
const s = SceneManager._scene._spriteset.children;
const child = s[s.length - 1].children;
const bitmap = child[child.length - 1].bitmap;
const ctx = bitmap._context;
ctx.strokeStyle = 'red';
ctx.fillStyle = 'blue';
ctx.strokeRect(0, 0, 200, 200);
